diff --git a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
index 5d63bb00f67..2f15c1f8a9b 100644
--- a/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
+++ b/docs/concepts/features/interfaces/prometheus.mdx
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ ClickHouse implements the Prometheus HTTP API over a [`TimeSeries`](/reference/e
 
 The query endpoints, remote write, and the `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions also accept a `Distributed` table over per-shard `TimeSeries` tables: samples are selected on every shard and the PromQL is evaluated on the initiator, and writes are routed through the `Distributed` sink. Remote read and the metadata endpoints (`/api/v1/series`, `/api/v1/labels`, `/api/v1/label/<name>/values`, `/api/v1/metadata`) remain `TimeSeries`-only and refuse a `Distributed` target.
 
-Every shard-local table behind such a `Distributed` table must be a `TimeSeries` table declaring the same `time_series` type as the `Distributed` table itself; a mismatch is refused rather than rounded, and the check asks every replica of every shard on every request, so a shard-local table swapped for another under the same name is caught by the next request rather than written into. A read is not failed by a replica the check cannot reach or that has no table of that name: whether the read itself fails is decided, as before, by its own `skip_unavailable_shards` settings. Remote write, however, is refused (with a retryable `5xx`) while any replica is unreachable, has no table of that name, or does not show that table's `time_series` type, because a replica the check could not see would still receive the samples unchecked. When the `Distributed` table declares no database, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on and parallel replicas off for these paths) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table; the query endpoints name the selector inside the `cluster()` call for each shard to resolve, so they need it only for a shard that is this server itself, together with `SELECT` on that shard's table; both are checked before the shard-local check runs too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table only.
+Every shard-local table behind such a `Distributed` table must be a `TimeSeries` table declaring the same `time_series` type as the `Distributed` table itself; a mismatch is refused rather than rounded, and the check asks every replica of every shard on every request, so a shard-local table swapped for another under the same name is caught by the next request rather than written into. A read is not failed by a replica the check cannot reach or that has no table of that name: whether the read itself fails is decided, as before, by its own `skip_unavailable_shards` settings. Remote write, however, is refused (with a retryable `5xx`) while any replica is unreachable, has no table of that name, or does not show that table's `time_series` type, because a replica the check could not see would still receive the samples unchecked. When the `Distributed` table declares no database, each shard resolves the name in its own default database, except a shard that is this server itself, which is always read and written in-process (`prefer_localhost_replica` is pinned on for both, and parallel replicas off for the reads) and so resolves it in the requesting user's current database, exactly as any query here would. Reading through it needs `SELECT` on the `Distributed` table and `READ ON REMOTE`, because the query is rewritten into a `cluster()` call; both are checked before the shard-local check above runs, so a caller without either is refused with a plain access error rather than told what the shards hold. The `prometheusQuery`/`prometheusQueryRange` table functions need `CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE` on top of those, as they do over a `TimeSeries` table; the query endpoints name the selector inside the `cluster()` call for each shard to resolve, so they need it only for a shard that is this server itself, together with `SELECT` on that shard's table; both are checked before the shard-local check runs too. Writing needs `INSERT` on the `Distributed` table only.
 
 Remote write into a `Distributed` target is an `INSERT` through that table's sink with [`distributed_foreground_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/distributed#distributed_foreground_insert) forced on and [`async_insert`](/reference/settings/session-settings/async-insert#async_insert) forced off, whatever the handler's user profile or the `remote_write` URL says: the samples are not queued on the initiator, by the sink or by the asynchronous insert queue, but delivered to the shards before the response, so a `204` means every shard (one replica per shard under `internal_replication`) has accepted them, and a shard that fails the insert fails the write with a retryable `5xx`, which Prometheus answers by resending the batch. A batch left in either queue would be flushed after the check above, into whatever answers to the shard-local name by then, which is why background delivery is not offered on this path. [`skip_unavailable_shards`](/reference/settings/session-settings/skip-unavailable-shards#skip_unavailable_shards) is forced off on this path too, whatever the URL or the profile says: a shard the sink skipped would be samples dropped under a `204`. The routing settings `insert_shard_id` and `insert_distributed_one_random_shard` are refused on this path, whether they come from the URL or from the profile: a batch goes where the table's sharding key sends it, so a `Distributed` table with several shards and no key cannot be written through remote write.
 
